   796	  # The ROOT is tested for being a symlink BEFORE it is resolved (rail round-2
   797	  # P1). The previous order asked `! -e` first, so only a DANGLING root was ever
   798	  # tested with `-L`: a RESOLVED symlink root answered `-e` true, fell through to
   799	  # `cd -P`, and silently moved the confinement BASE to the referent. MEASURED:
   800	  # with the target passed as a symlink to a real directory, the install ran to
   801	  # completion, 567 files landed under the referent and zero landed anywhere
   802	  # else — so containment held, but the comment here claimed a refusal that the
   803	  # code did not perform, and `install.sh` resolves the target with plain
   804	  # `cd`/`pwd` (LOGICAL path, symlink preserved), so the case is reachable on
   805	  # every real install.
   806	  #
   807	  # It is a REFUSAL, not a warning, and the reason carries the recovery: the
   808	  # operator re-runs against the referent. That keeps the gate fail-closed while
   809	  # costing a legitimate symlinked project directory exactly one re-run with the
   810	  # path the message prints — and it makes the dereference an explicit choice
   811	  # instead of something the installer did on the operator's behalf without
   812	  # saying so. Note `-L` tests only the LAST component, so an ordinary path that
   813	  # merely traverses a symlinked ancestor (/tmp -> /private/tmp on macOS) is
   814	  # unaffected.
