     3	# INERT AS SHIPPED. install.sh copies this file to
     4	# `.github/workflows/benchmarks.yml.template` — the `.template` suffix means
     5	# GitHub Actions never reads it. Activation is an EXPLICIT adopter step
     6	# (plain `mv -n`, not `git mv`: on a fresh install the delivered file is
     7	# UNTRACKED so `git mv` fails — measured S344, PLAN-183 AC-2 — and `-n`
     8	# refuses to clobber a benchmarks.yml of your own — but on that collision
     9	# `mv -n` exits 0 WITHOUT moving, so the workflow stays INERT: check the
    10	# rename landed, or pick another name, before you commit):
    11	#   mv -n .github/workflows/benchmarks.yml.template \
    12	#         .github/workflows/benchmarks.yml
    13	#
    14	# ONCE ACTIVATED THIS COSTS REAL MONEY ON YOUR OWN ACCOUNT. It requires the
    15	# repository secret ANTHROPIC_API_KEY (Settings -> Secrets and variables ->
    16	# Actions). Each run bills YOUR Anthropic account: one SUCCESSFUL response per
    17	# benchmark scenario per repetition — the runner makes up to three request
    18	# attempts on a rate limit or API error, so request volume can exceed that
    19	# count — for every benchmark YAML under
    20	# `.claude/skills/**/benchmarks/`. The runner refuses any single benchmark
    21	# YAML whose ESTIMATED cost exceeds $1.00 unless invoked with
    22	# --allow-expensive — a per-invocation cap, not a budget; there is NO
    23	# aggregate workflow-wide cap, and the paths filter below is the only
    24	# throttle on how often the whole job runs. With no secret configured the
    25	# job skips visibly and bills nothing.
    26	#
    27	# SELF-CONTAINED BY CONTRACT (PLAN-183 W2 / finding A3): every script this
    28	# workflow calls must be one the installer actually delivers.
    29	# `.claude/scripts/run-skill-benchmark.py` ships via
    30	# install.sh::install_scripts_selective(). The framework's own
    31	# `.github/scripts/` is NOT installed, so nothing here may reference it.
    32	#
    33	# Only runs on changes to skill benchmarks, the runner, or this workflow
    34	# itself. Per PLAN-002 §11-bis Q2: paths filter keeps docs/plans/hooks PRs
    35	# from paying API cost.
    36	on:
    37	  pull_request:
    38	    paths:
    39	      - ".claude/skills/**/benchmarks/**"
    40	      - ".claude/scripts/run-skill-benchmark.py"
    41	      - ".github/workflows/benchmarks.yml"
    42	  push:
    43	    branches: [main]
    44	    paths:
    45	      - ".claude/skills/**/benchmarks/**"
    46	      - ".claude/scripts/run-skill-benchmark.py"
    47	      - ".github/workflows/benchmarks.yml"
    48	
    49	concurrency:
    50	  group: benchmarks-${{ github.ref }}
    51	  cancel-in-progress: true
    52	
    53	permissions:
    54	  contents: read
    55	  # Write permission needed for $GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY
    56	  pull-requests: write
    57	
    58	jobs:
    59	  benchmarks:
    60	    name: Run skill benchmarks
    61	    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    62	    timeout-minutes: 10
    63	
    64	    # Fork PR safety: benchmarks require ANTHROPIC_API_KEY which fork PRs
    65	    # don't have access to. Skip visibly on forks. pull_request_target is
    66	    # EXPLICITLY FORBIDDEN per PLAN-002 §8 finding #18 (code-injection
    67	    # vector in the forked repo's workflow).
    68	    if: github.event_name != 'pull_request' || github.event.pull_request.head.repo.full_name == github.repository
    69	
    70	    steps:
    71	      - name: Checkout
    72	        uses: actions/checkout@v4
    73	
    74	      - name: Set up Python
    75	        uses: actions/setup-python@v5
    76	        with:
    77	          python-version: "3.12"
    78	
    79	      - name: Install dependencies
    80	        run: |
    81	          set -euo pipefail
    82	          python3 -m pip install --upgrade pip
    83	          python3 -m pip install pyyaml anthropic
    84	
    85	      - name: Detect changed benchmarks
    86	        id: detect
    87	        run: |
    88	          set -euo pipefail
    89	          # Find all benchmark YAMLs under skills/
    90	          mapfile -t ALL_BENCHES < <(find .claude/skills -type f -path '*/benchmarks/*.yaml' 2>/dev/null)
    91	          if [ ${#ALL_BENCHES[@]} -eq 0 ]; then
    92	            echo "no_benchmarks=true" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
    93	            echo "No benchmark YAMLs found — nothing to run" >&2
    94	          else
    95	            printf 'found %d benchmark file(s):\n' "${#ALL_BENCHES[@]}"
    96	            printf '  %s\n' "${ALL_BENCHES[@]}"
    97	          fi
    98	
    99	      - name: Run benchmarks (advisory, skip-if-no-key)
   100	        id: bench
   101	        env:
   102	          ANTHROPIC_API_KEY: ${{ secrets.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY }}
   103	        run: |
   104	          set -euo pipefail
   105	          mkdir -p benchmark-results
   106	
   107	          if [ -z "${ANTHROPIC_API_KEY:-}" ]; then
   108	            echo "SKIPPED: ANTHROPIC_API_KEY secret not set (advisory mode)." >&2
   109	            echo "skipped=true" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
   110	            exit 0
   111	          fi
   112	
   113	          FAILED=0
   114	          mapfile -t ALL_BENCHES < <(find .claude/skills -type f -path '*/benchmarks/*.yaml')
   115	          # Sprint 3 Item B: absolute floor at 0.6 + write lessons on failure.
   116	          # Per debate consensus R-DEV1, rc=1 for ALL floor failures (no new
   117	          # exit codes — we distinguish via $GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY, not rc).
   118	          for bench in "${ALL_BENCHES[@]}"; do
   119	            base=$(basename "$bench" .yaml)
   120	            echo "::group::Running $bench"
   121	            out="benchmark-results/${base}.json"
   122	            md="benchmark-results/${base}.md"
   123	            # `$?` inside `if ! cmd` is the status of `!` (always 0), so the
   124	            # floor breach was reported as "runner error rc=0": capture the
   125	            # real status first, then branch on it.
   126	            rc=0
   127	            python3 .claude/scripts/run-skill-benchmark.py \
   128	                 "$bench" \
   129	                 --output-json "$out" \
   130	                 --markdown \
   131	                 --floor 0.6 \
   132	                 --write-lessons \
   133	                 --skip-if-no-key > "$md" || rc=$?
   134	            if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
   135	              if [ "$rc" -eq 1 ]; then
   136	                echo "::error::$bench below floor (CRITICAL or <0.6)"
   137	                FAILED=1
   138	              else
   139	                echo "::error::$bench: runner error rc=$rc"
   140	                FAILED=1
   141	              fi
   142	            fi
   143	            if [ -s "$md" ]; then cat "$md"; fi
   144	            echo "::endgroup::"
   145	          done
   146	
   147	          echo "failed=$FAILED" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
   148	
   149	      - name: Post results to $GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY
   150	        if: always()
   151	        run: |
   152	          set -euo pipefail
   153	          if [ "${{ steps.bench.outputs.skipped }}" = "true" ]; then
   154	            {
   155	              printf '## Benchmarks skipped\n\n'
   156	              printf 'ANTHROPIC_API_KEY secret is not configured.\n'
   157	              printf 'Enable in Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Actions.\n'
   158	            } >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"
   159	            exit 0
   160	          fi
   161	          if [ ! -d benchmark-results ] || [ -z "$(ls -A benchmark-results 2>/dev/null)" ]; then
   162	            printf '## Benchmarks: no results produced\n' >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"
   163	            exit 0
   164	          fi
   165	          # PLAN-183 W2 / A3: the summary is built from the per-benchmark
   166	          # Markdown that .claude/scripts/run-skill-benchmark.py emits with
   167	          # --markdown (captured per bench in the step above). The framework's
   168	          # .github/scripts/ is NOT installed, so it must not be called here.
   169	          if ls benchmark-results/*.md >/dev/null 2>&1; then
   170	            cat benchmark-results/*.md >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"
   171	          else
   172	            printf '## Benchmarks: results produced but no Markdown captured\n' \
   173	              >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"
   174	          fi
   175	
   176	      - name: Upload benchmark JSON results
   177	        if: always()
   178	        uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
   179	        with:
   180	          name: benchmark-results
   181	          path: benchmark-results/
   182	          if-no-files-found: ignore
   183	          retention-days: 30
   184	
   185	      - name: Fail on floor breach
   186	        if: steps.bench.outputs.failed == '1'
   187	        run: |
   188	          echo "::error::One or more benchmarks fell below floor (CRITICAL < 0.4 or absolute < 0.6)"
   189	          exit 1
     1	<!--
     2	  compaction.md — editable nine-section conversation-compaction template
     3	  (PLAN-133 / Wave D / item D4 — context management)
     4	
     5	  PURPOSE
     6	    When a session approaches its context budget and must be compacted, the
     7	    model is asked to rewrite the transcript-so-far into a single dense
     8	    summary. An UNSTRUCTURED summary drops load-bearing state (open blockers,
     9	    env flags, staged canonical edits). This template fixes the OUTPUT SHAPE
    10	    so nothing critical is lost.
    11	
    12	  HOW IT IS USED
    13	    This is an EDITABLE, NON-CANONICAL framework template. It is plain
    14	    Markdown — copy it into a target repo (or keep it here for the meta-repo),
    15	    then EXTEND the sections for your domain (extend, never trim — see the
    16	    sharp edge in "How this template is actually used" below). Feed the
    17	    rendered template as the compaction instruction; the model fills each of
    18	    the nine sections from the live transcript. Empty sections must be emitted
    19	    as the literal "(none)" so a reader can tell "nothing here" from "the
    20	    model forgot".
    21	
    22	  DELIVERY CHANNEL — USE DOCTRINE (PLAN-179 W3 US10, declared S334)
    23	    The consumer of this template is the OPERATOR, through the interactive
    24	    `/compact <instructions>` command of the CLI, in long sessions that are
    25	    about to compact and carry load-bearing state. It is deliberately NOT
    26	    wired to the API `instructions` parameter of `compact_20260112`: that
    27	    parameter REPLACES the default compaction prompt wholesale
    28	    (PLAN-179/research-S309.md §1), so an incomplete rendering there is
    29	    strictly worse than the default — omission becomes recall loss. The
    30	    mechanical half of continuity does not live here at all: constraint
    31	    re-injection travels through its own channel (pinned constraints via
    32	    SessionStart(matcher=compact) + PostCompact, PLAN-179 W1-b), which does
    33	    not depend on the operator remembering this template.
    34	
    35	    WHO FEEDS IT: an operator, manually. Nothing in this framework feeds this
    36	    template automatically, and inside Claude Code nothing can — the routes
    37	    and the local evidence are evaluated in the first section of the body
    38	    (PLAN-179 W3 / US10). The earlier claim that the "D1 auto-compact policy
    39	    in scripts/context-budget.py" drives this template was never true: that
    40	    probe has no caller anywhere in the repo (see the same plan, US11).
    41	
    42	  DESIGN RULES (so the summary survives a fresh session boot)
    43	    1. Every section header below is STABLE — do not rename them; downstream
    44	       readers and any future parser key off the exact "## N. <Title>" line.
    45	    2. Preserve EXACT identifiers verbatim: file paths (absolute), commit
    46	       SHAs, PLAN-/ADR- ids, env-flag names, closed-enum audit actions.
    47	       Never paraphrase an identifier — a summary that says "the audit hook"
    48	       instead of ".claude/hooks/audit_log.py" is lossy.
    49	    3. NEVER echo a secret value. If a credential/token/key appeared in the
    50	       transcript, record only that it appeared and was handled — never the
    51	       value itself (mirrors the framework's no-value-echo audit doctrine).
    52	    4. Prefer bullets over prose. Compaction is for density, not narrative.
    53	    5. If a fact is uncertain, mark it "(unverified)" rather than asserting it.
    54	
    55	  CONTAMINATION NOTE
    56	    This template ships in the framework core (templates/). Do NOT hardcode
    57	    personal handles, real names, employer names, or adopter project names
    58	    here. Use the literal placeholder @OWNER for the maintainer and
    59	    <project> for the target repo. The contamination scanner
    60	    (.claude/scripts/check_contamination.py) gates this file.
    61	-->
    62	
    63	## How this template is actually used
    64	
    65	> **Verdict (PLAN-179 W3 / US10, recorded 2026-08-18).** This template is
    66	> **not** wired to anything automatically, and — inside Claude Code — it
    67	> **cannot be**. It is fed by a human, on one route only. The two candidate
    68	> routes were evaluated against what this repository can actually verify
    69	> locally; the evidence for each is named below. Do not read this section as
    70	> a promise of wiring: it is the honest boundary.
    71	
    72	### Route (a) — `/compact <instructions>` in the CLI · **USABLE, MANUAL ONLY**
    73	
    74	Typing `/compact` followed by instruction text hands that text to the
    75	harness's summarizer. Local evidence that the harness carries such a field:
    76	the pinned hook schema `.claude/data/hook-schema-2.1.220.json` declares
    77	
    78	    "PreCompact": { "trigger": "enum[manual,auto]",
    79	                    "custom_instructions": "string|null" }
    80	
    81	so a manual compaction genuinely transports operator-authored instructions.
    82	
    83	Two consequences follow from the SAME schema file, and both are load-bearing:
    84	
    85	1. **No hook can supply this template.** The schema's `_absent_arms_note`
    86	   lists `PreCompact` among the events with **no `hookSpecificOutput` arm** —
    87	   there is no `additionalContext`, and no field a hook may return to SET
    88	   `custom_instructions`. A `PreCompact` hook may observe the compaction, and
    89	   may BLOCK it (exit 2), but it cannot author the instruction. Automation of
    90	   this route is therefore not available; the operator pastes the template.
    91	2. **Auto-compaction gets nothing.** On `trigger = auto`,
    92	   `custom_instructions` is `null` — nobody typed anything. The auto path is
    93	   the DOMINANT path for long sessions (the exact finding recorded against
    94	   ADR-153), so this template covers the case that happens least often.
    95	
    96	### Route (b) — the API `instructions` parameter · **ADOPTER-ONLY, UNVERIFIED HERE**
    97	
    98	The server-side compaction tool takes an `instructions` string that replaces
    99	the default summarization prompt. Inside this repository that route is
   100	**documented but not exercised**: the only local description lives in
   101	`.claude/plans/PLAN-179/research-S309.md §1.1` (a research note citing the
   102	vendor docs — external content, recorded as DATA), and a repo-wide grep for
   103	the tool identifier and for `context_management` finds **no caller** — only
   104	plan and research prose. This framework installs into a Claude Code harness;
   105	it does not own an API request loop, so it cannot exercise route (b) at all.
   106	
   107	Route (b) is real for an **adopter** who owns their own API loop. For them,
   108	rendering this template into `instructions` is the wiring. For this
   109	repository, treat it as unverified-locally: mark any claim about it
   110	`(unverified)` until a probe demonstrates it end-to-end.
   111	
   112	### THE SHARP EDGE — `instructions` REPLACES, it does not append
   113	
   114	On route (b) the `instructions` string **substitutes the default
   115	summarization prompt in its entirety**. Nothing is merged and nothing warns
   116	you. Any recall the default prompt would have produced, and that this
   117	template does not explicitly ask for, is **silently lost** — the summary
   118	looks complete and is not. The same hazard applies in spirit to route (a):
   119	whatever you type steers the summarizer away from its default behaviour.
   120	
   121	Therefore:
   122	
   123	- Send the template **whole**. Trimming a section for brevity is deleting a
   124	  recall requirement, not saving tokens.
   125	- When you add a domain section, **add** it — never replace one of the nine.
   126	- A section with nothing to report is emitted as the literal `(none)`. That
   127	  is what distinguishes "nothing here" from "the instruction did not ask".
   128	- After a compaction driven by this template, spot-check that all nine
   129	  headers came back. A missing header is evidence of instruction loss, not
   130	  of an empty session.
   131	
   132	### Status of the automatic route
   133	
   134	**BLOCKED ON A PROBE.** No automatic route exists today, and route (a) is
   135	structurally closed to automation for the reason given above. Any future
   136	claim that this template feeds compaction automatically must cite a probe
   137	that (i) emits a unique canary through the chosen channel and (ii) FAILS when
   138	the canary is withheld — a positive control. Until such a probe exists, the
   139	honest description of this file is: *a manual instruction the operator
   140	pastes, plus a shape contract the nine sections below define.*
   141	
   142	---
   143	
   144	# Session Compaction Summary — `<project>` @ `<session-id>`
   145	
   146	> Compacted at: `<UTC timestamp>` · Trigger: `<manual | auto-compact>` ·
   147	> Pre-compaction context: `<NN%>` of budget.
   148	> Replace every `<...>` placeholder. Emit `(none)` for any section with no
   149	> content — never delete a section header.
   150	
   151	---
